// automations.memql -- the __PRODUCT__ product pack (starter). Pure DSL, no Go.
//
// One event-driven automation that reacts to a graph lifecycle event and runs a
// pure-DSL logic step -- ZERO product Go, no integration executor. It hooks the
// pack's OWN greeting concept so the starter tree is self-contained (it resolves
// under dslimports.Load / memqllint with no core namespace present). A real pack
// often hooks a CORE service event instead
// (@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:<core>:<concept>", ...)) to react
// to the engine's own rows -- the core has no knowledge of the pack.
//
// The step calls __PRODUCT_CAMEL__OnGreetingCreated (logic.memql) with the triggering node bound
// as `event`. That body reads the greetings owned by the NEW greeting's owner --
// passing the owner id from event.payload.ownerUserId, because an automation
// runs under a SYSTEM actor (so actor.userId is NOT the owner). This exercises
// the full event -> automation -> logic -> query path on a plain engine with no
// product code. The trigger names the concept by its canonical id STRING; that
// is not a DSL import, so no `use` of the concept is needed here.
//
// The step passes the event to the logic with a named argument
// (`react := logic <name>(event: event)`) and references the logic by its bare,
// un-prefixed name (resolved through the file-top `use`) -- the canonical
// automation-step grammar (see the engine's authoring-rules.md).

use __PRODUCT_ID__.logic.{ __PRODUCT_CAMEL__OnGreetingCreated }

@trigger(event="node.created", concept="v1:__PRODUCT_ID__:greeting")
@description("On creation of a greeting, run the pure-DSL __PRODUCT_CAMEL__OnGreetingCreated logic. Demonstrates a pack automation firing on a graph event with zero product Go.")
automation __PRODUCT_CAMEL__GreetOnGreetingCreate {
  react := logic __PRODUCT_CAMEL__OnGreetingCreated(event: event)
}
